# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored: never commit real values.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS FIRST: there is no Firebase private key in this file any more.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The Admin SDK authenticates with Application Default Credentials (ADC). You
# sign in once as yourself and Google holds the credential outside the repo:
#
#     gcloud auth application-default login
#
# That is the whole setup. FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID below is the only Firebase
# server variable you need.
#
# Do NOT paste a service-account private key here, and do not download a
# service-account JSON key to make it work. A key in a .env file is a
# long-lived credential with full Admin SDK power, which bypasses every
# Firestore rule. It gets copied between laptops, pasted into chats, and
# committed by accident, and nothing expires it. On Cloud Run the runtime
# service account already holds roles/datastore.user in the same project, so
# there is no key to store in Secret Manager, none to rotate, and none to leak.
#
# The service-account pair still works, for the case ADC cannot cover: running
# against a Firestore project your Google account has no access to. If you
# genuinely need it, see the commented block further down, and set BOTH halves.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The other rule: NEXT_PUBLIC_ is a publication decision, not a naming style.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Next inlines every NEXT_PUBLIC_* value into the browser bundle at build time.
# Anything carrying that prefix is public, permanently, to anyone who opens
# devtools. A secret never gets that prefix. The Firebase web config below is
# an exception only because it is designed to be public: it identifies the
# project, it does not authorise anything, and Firestore rules are what
# actually guard the data.

# --- Firebase web config (public by design, safe in the browser) ---
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=your_api_key
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=your-project.firebaseapp.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your-project-id
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=your-project.appspot.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=your_sender_id
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=your_app_id
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=your_measurement_id

# --- Firebase Admin SDK (server only) ---
#
# Required either way. ADC could infer the project from the metadata server,
# but requiring it keeps "no config at all" a loud error rather than silently
# authenticating against whatever project your gcloud happens to point at.
# The yard satellite reuses this variable name, so one file can feed both.
FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your-project-id

# Fallback only, for a Firestore project your Google account cannot reach.
# Set both or neither: exactly one half set is refused at startup, because
# falling back to ADC would silently authenticate as a different identity.
# Prefer ADC. If you do fill these in, treat the key as a live credential.
# FIREBASE_CLIENT_EMAIL=firebase-adminsdk-xxxxx@your-project-id.iam.gserviceaccount.com
# FIREBASE_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nYOUR_PRIVATE_KEY\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"

# --- Which deployment this is ---
#
# "prod", "staging", or unset for local development. Drives the environment
# banner and tags analytics. No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix on purpose: the prod deploy
# promotes the exact image digest built for staging rather than rebuilding, and
# a build-time value would permanently label production as staging. Terraform
# sets it per Cloud Run service; leave it unset locally.
# APP_ENV=staging

# --- Public origin, for absolute links in outgoing email ---
#
# APP_URL is the runtime value and wins. NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL is the local-dev
# fallback and is frozen at build time, which is exactly why it cannot be the
# one prod relies on. See infrastructure/config/appUrl.ts.
# APP_URL=https://marsyard.sapient.rocks
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Which yard missions are submitted to ---
#
# Defaults to the yard registry in infrastructure/config/yards. Override only
# when pointing a local app at a different yard. Must match YARD_ID on the
# satellite, or the satellite pulls an empty queue and logs nothing to say why.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_YARD_ID=curiosity

# --- Resend (server only, DO NOT COMMIT real values) ---
# Used by MissionNotificationService to send mission status-change emails.
RESEND_API_KEY=your_resend_api_key
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=missions@sapient.rocks

# Sandbox only. Until a domain is verified at resend.com/domains,
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL has to stay onboarding@resend.dev, and Resend rejects every
# recipient except the address that owns the API key. Setting this redirects all
# outgoing mail to that one permitted inbox (the intended recipient is prefixed
# into the subject) so the pipeline can be demoed end to end. Leave it UNSET
# once the domain is verified: with it set, no learner ever receives mail.
# RESEND_SANDBOX_RECIPIENT=owner_of_the_resend_account@example.com

# --- PostHog (public project key, safe in the browser) ---
# Powers autocapture, session replay, and rage/dead/error click detection, see
# PostHogProvider. One project covers every environment; events are tagged with
# the runtime-resolved APP_ENV rather than a separate key per environment, so
# leave this unset if you do not want local dev traffic showing up in PostHog.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=phc_your_project_api_key
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# --- If your .env predates this file ---
#
# SIMULATOR_SERVICE_URL and YARD_SERVER_URL are read by nothing in the repo.
# Delete them. FIREBASE_CLIENT_EMAIL and FIREBASE_PRIVATE_KEY should come out
# too once `gcloud auth application-default login` works for you, and the key
# they name should then be deleted in the Google Cloud console rather than just
# removed from the file: a key that still exists is still a way in.
